Dependency injection in C# is when a class *asks for* what it needs instead of *creating it itself*. That makes the code cleaner, easier to test, and easier to swap out later[1][5].

In gangster terms: **“Don’t let the class run its own errands.”** If `Car` needs an `Engine`, you don’t have `Car` sneak off and build one in the back alley. You hand it the engine from outside[1][5].

```csharp
public interface IEngine
{
    void Start();
}

public class V8Engine : IEngine
{
    public void Start() => Console.WriteLine("Engine purring.");
}

public class Car
{
    private readonly IEngine _engine;

    public Car(IEngine engine)
    {
        _engine = engine;
    }

    public void Drive()
    {
        _engine.Start();
        Console.WriteLine("Car is rolling.");
    }
}
```

Here, `Car` **depends on** `IEngine`, but it does not create `V8Engine` itself. Something else—usually a DI container—provides it at runtime[1][5].

Why this is powerful:

- **Swappable parts:** You can replace `V8Engine` with `ElectricEngine` without changing `Car`[4][5].
- **Better testing:** In tests, you can inject a fake engine instead of the real one[2][4].
- **Less coupling:** Classes stay focused on their own job instead of building their dependencies too[2][5].

The most common style in C# is **constructor injection**, where the dependency is passed through the constructor[2][5]. Microsoft’s .NET DI container supports this pattern directly[5].

